eac2eed4b666c7ca9eab6f6ba7d3bc161387433e.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "321fa992-8b54-51c4-9c6a-89b1f36a3444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167094 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our ellipse is centered at $(2,-1)$ and has semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)}{9}+\\frac{2(y+1)}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2(x-2)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{16(x-2)}{9(y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167096 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/88/88bec66b7ece961f2f0fc4083ce317f2bdc414e0a219d61bbf9bf73ae667ca0f.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b2fc0d7-ecff-5b63-8ad8-a6faaff8e7b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167096 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-2,2).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$ and $y=2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167750 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167750 #edb/ref 1061 35885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167750 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6af5a081-1dac-5c00-aecb-60e48eb5a180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167750 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167751 #edb/ref 1061 35886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32f917dc-ab34-51bf-8295-5111957c7a29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167752 #edb/ref 1061 35887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd16ea49-d32a-5632-8ad6-d600c7a64959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167753 #edb/ref 1061 35888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7dfe24c-f0a2-5e29-a4f6-6589521d6e34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168125 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the circle below. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ the slope of the tangent to this circle at any point on the circle? \n\n![](images/4c/4cfdf4a02935f6a729b12a81a5b48d0356e37046fb5dabfdeefc498a68b8ed8d.png)\n\nTo calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$, we start by writing down the equation of the circle. Then, we differentiate the equation of the circle with respect to $x.$\n\nSince our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $\\sqrt{5},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = (\\sqrt{5})^2 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + y^2 = 5.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ we differentiate the equation of the circle using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the slope at any point on the circle using our expression for the derivative. \n\nFor example, since the point $P\\big( \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3} \\big)$ satisfies the equation $x^2 + y^2 = 5,$ it must lie on the circle. Therefore, the slope of the tangent at this point is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\Bigg|_{( \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3})} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168125 #edb/ref 1065 5865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168125 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69315ade-3415-5abd-94e7-90d616fb7002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168125 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260095 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{y}{2x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260095 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260095 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "193eadf3-4d9d-5560-876d-95d6202e9c93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260357 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x}{4y}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260357 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260357 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35d44e75-ae76-5b88-839e-6d033a89f631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921